# Sway configuration.
#
# This file defines variables and loads everything else from config.d/.
# One concern per fragment; see man 5 sway for the directives themselves.

### Variables

# Logo key. Use Mod1 for Alt.
set $mod Mod4

# Home row direction keys, like vim.
set $left h
set $down j
set $up k
set $right l

# Not bare `foot`: every terminal opens with a system summary, which is a
# property of opening a terminal rather than of starting a shell - see the
# script for why that distinction matters. Called by path, since ~/.local/bin
# is not on the PATH sway hands to what it spawns.
set $term ~/.local/bin/terminal

# TWO FILE MANAGERS, DELIBERATELY AND ON A CLOCK - see TASK-189, and TASK-190
# which ends this. The same bargain as the two browsers above and the two
# Escape keys in the keyd config: two things doing one job is what this
# repository argues against, and it is tolerable only while the ticket that
# removes one of them exists.
#
# $explorer is on $mod+e, $explorer_tui on $mod+Ctrl+e. Both are on a key
# rather than one being demoted to the launcher, because TASK-44 settled the
# last file-manager question on REACHABILITY - nothing routed to Thunar, so
# a fortnight of use could only have recorded someone typing its name. A
# comparison needs both to be genuinely reachable or it measures nothing.
#
# Thunar takes no app_id flag and thunar.desktop ships no StartupWMClass, so
# the app_id is whatever GTK derives at runtime. It is `thunar`, lowercase,
# read out of `swaymsg -t get_tree` with a window open rather than assumed -
# 40-window-rules.conf depends on that string and nothing notices if it is
# wrong except the window failing to float.
set $explorer thunar
# The terminal one is a terminal with a distinct app_id - otherwise the window
# rule that floats it would float every terminal.
set $explorer_tui foot --app-id=explorer -e yazi
# The helper chooses WHICH browser, not how to open it. It is firefox, since
# TASK-183 - with Vimium force-installed by policy, so the keyboard-driven
# browsing qutebrowser was picked for in the first place comes with it. TASK-178
# put the choice behind a one-line state file and this key follows it, so
# `browser --use vimb` and `browser --use qutebrowser` still switch and
# `browser --current` says which is in force.
#
# Firefox is the slower of the two, measured rather than assumed: TASK-183 got
# 862ms against qutebrowser's 363ms warm, and 875ms against 803ms cold from
# disk. That is the known price of this decision, paid once per window, and
# TASK-183 judged it smaller than reaching for a second browser when a site does
# not render. Do not quote TASK-177's 1673ms here - it is a different cache
# state and these two numbers are not comparable to it.
#
# Each launch still opens a new WINDOW rather than a tab in the window you
# used last. Each browser gets there differently: qutebrowser through
# `new_instance_open_target` in dot_config/qutebrowser/config.py, firefox
# through a `--new-window` the helper adds, and vimb through being one process
# per invocation already. Set in each browser rather than as a flag here, so
# the launcher and the terminal behave the same way as this key.
#
# Links from OTHER applications deliberately do not go through this - they go
# to firefox.desktop and land in a tab. See
# dotfiles/run_onchange_after_set-default-applications.sh.
set $browser ~/.local/bin/browser
# One prompt, several sources. `combi` merges the modes listed in
# config.rasi - applications, open windows, files, calculator - so there is a
# single thing to reach for rather than one launcher per kind of question.
set $menu rofi -show combi

### Configuration

# System fragments shipped by packages load first, so that anything this
# repository sets always takes precedence over a distribution default.
include /etc/sway/config.d/*

include config.d/*.conf
